{"title": "Research: crewmate refused to run gh on the user's request", "goal": "Establish from the code and prompts why a crewmate refused the user's request to run the GitHub CLI to upload release files. Find which instruction or rule says crewmates must not use another signed-in program, and check that the built-in GitHub connection truly can't upload release assets. Show the answer on one drawn card with citations, with recommended fixes for both the missing upload and the refusal.", "userWords": "investigate another user report. the missing api is one thing, but also the user ended up asking the agent to use gh cli as work around, but the agent refused. i don't think we should ever refuse to run programs on user request\n\nThread\n\nyou\nwhy cant you do this autonmously?\n\n07:46 PM\ncrew\nYou already authorized publication; this is a capability limit, not another approval requirement. Firstmate’s GitHub connection can edit release details but cannot send release files, and its browser cannot select local files. I’m not permitted to bypass that limit by using your GitHub sign-in through another program.\n07:46 PM\nyou\nwhy is this a limitation in fm3k but not in regular firstmate?\n07:47 PM\napp\nrevised: FM3K's current access cannot upload release files07:48 PM\nTitle\nChange 1\nWhy FM3K is blocked here\n1 part removed\ncrew\nFM3K requires me to use its built-in GitHub connection, which does not expose file uploads, and prohibits switching to another authenticated program. That combination blocks autonomous uploads here, despite your publication authorization. I haven’t verified regular Firstmate’s upload mechanism or why FM3K chose this boundary, so I can’t honestly claim the exact implementation difference.\n07:48 PM\n[truncated 0 more characters; full text: ~/Library/Application Support/infinitary/overflow/pasted-text-e3122743.txt]", "constraints": "read-only; free: Read the whole pasted thread first: ~/Library/Application Support/infinitary/overflow/pasted-text-e3122743.txt. Find the exact crewmate instructions, tool descriptions or host guards behind \"prohibits switching to another authenticated program\", and say whether a host check would also block a gh call, not only the prompt. Check whether the forge layer supports uploading release assets. Weigh the captain's stated principle, \"never refuse to run programs on user request\", against the existing safety rules, such as never putting secrets on a command line and keychain limits. Recommend the change to make.", "expectedKind": "research", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-205 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Research: GitHub read saves a temporary credential locally", "goal": "Establish from the code whether a crewmate's GitHub read of a private repository can return a temporary credential (such as a tokenized download or raw URL) that then gets saved locally, and where. Say whether current Infinitary still does this, and recommend the fix, on one drawn card with citations.", "userWords": "investigate another user report: Hi! While using Firstmate 3000 (0.4.0) I came across a small security issue: a crewmate's GitHub read of a private repository can return a temporary credential, which then gets saved locally. I have a short write-up and a tested patch with a test if needed.", "constraints": "read-only; free: Trace the github_read tool and the forge's read path for private repositories. Look for responses carrying temporary tokens, such as download_url or raw URLs with ?token=, or signed archive and asset links, and find where those results are persisted: session transcripts, task reports, evidence or the knowledge store. Check both the firstmate-3000 0.4.0 era and the current code. Never print a real token, and never run Keychain or security commands against the real keychain. If the reporter's write-up and patch would help confirm or fix it, say so on the card.", "expectedKind": "research", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-206 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
