{"title": "Study rigid logic in Infinitary and design composable skills", "goal": "Find every recent case in the captain's Infinitary sessions where rigid code blocked what he wanted, and propose an architecture to prevent it. The architecture turns hard-coded rules into composable tools, scripts, skills and instructions. It also assesses modeling sequences, lessons, casual projects and similar behaviors as skill packs that can be turned on and off, shared and installed.", "userWords": "i want an astra crewmate to study a few architectural questions - i noticed recently we had a few issues with rigidness in infinitary preventing our agents from doing what the user actually want. e.g. a crewmate on project A can't raise a PR normally for project B. an admin merge couldn't be made because our code didn't allow it. and many many more examples like that. study recent sessions i had in infinitary should be able to reveal a lot of them. i want the crewmate to find all the recent instances where rigid logic caused user inconvenience, and figure out a better architecture we can establish to prevent such problems. what i'm thinking is - we should establish some principles such as turning a lot of these rigid code into tools / scripts / skills / instructions etc but ultimately the agent can compose them in different ways to achieve different things. related, i also wonder whether things like how we handle \"sequences\", lessons, casual projects etc can be modeled as \"skill packs\". then potentially down the road we can allow users to turn each of them on/off depending on their need, and we can allow people to share and install skill packs from each other that would give them more capabilities like how we do lessons, casual projects etc. this should be a thorough and thoughtful architectural design, not a quick answer", "constraints": "read-only; free: Cite each instance found in the session records and task history, with the code that enforced it. Related existing work to re