{"title": "Why PR #268's Mac journeys ran 22 minutes", "goal": "Explain with evidence why the affected-journeys job for PR #268 on the Mac mini ran 22 minutes after INF-659 (PR #204) added a 6-minute Mac budget meant to keep most pull requests' journeys under 10 minutes, and name the fix.", "userWords": "i thought we recently made a change to control these journey runs to be under 10m by forcing a budget. why it still takes much more than that?", "constraints": "read-only; free: Start from INF-659's report and PR #204. Read PR #268's journeys run: how many journeys it picked, its time budget against the time actually spent, any wait for the Mac check lock before it started, setup time, retries, and whether the budget even applied. Also check other recent runs to show whether 22 minutes is typical. Never open fm-runner.pat or any secret.", "expectedKind": "research", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-763 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Audit card actions and propose a clearer design", "goal": "Audit every way the captain acts on a card: reply, close, minimize, buttons, the last card on screen, and whether a card comes back. Record what each does today, with file paths, and where it surprises him. Then propose two or three clearer designs as one card, with the one you would pick as the default.", "userWords": "We have a usability problem that I want fable crewmate to look at. It's on each card that we show. Some users complained that it's not very clear what will happen when they do certain actions, such as replying to the card. It will close the card in most cases unless the card is the last one on the screen. And then when that's the case and they reply, they are not sure whether they can just close the card and whether it will come back or not. I think now with the minimize action it's a little bit improved, but I'm still not sure whether everything is very intuitive to users. I want the crewmates to audit this whole user experience and propose whether there is a good adjustment we can make, any good experiences we can replace current solution with that will make everything very intuitive", "constraints": "read-only", "expectedKind": "plan", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-770 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Assess the first mate's final words as its reply above input", "goal": "Map today's rules for the short messages above the main message box: what shows, when, and how it fades, with file paths. Then judge whether showing the first mate's final text of each turn, kept short by instruction alone, would make every captain ask end with a clear reply saying what it did. Propose two or three directions as one card, with the one you would pick as the default.", "userWords": "Another user experience question I want fable crewmate to look at. It's with the little chat messages that we show right above the main chat input. Right now, I think we have some rules on what will show up and how it will fade. I'm wondering whether we should actually lean on the firstmate agent's text response, the final text response in each turn to be the message that we show as the agent response. That will make everything more consistent with how the model is trained. And I think we should restrict the final text response to a certain length so that it doesn't end up being too long. And that should be done by instruction only. Have the crewmates look into this idea and assess whether we can indeed drive an improvement through that. The goal is that every user ask will end up seeing a response that explains what firstmate actually did. So the user is very clear on what's happening.", "constraints": "read-only; free: A related audit of card actions (INF-770, task 517fb99c) is running at the same time; read it if useful, but do not depend on it.", "expectedKind": "plan", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-772 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Design the integrations primitive, starting with Gmail and Calen", "goal": "Design Infinitary's integrations primitive thoroughly, with GitHub as the reference, and Gmail and Google Calendar as the first new ones. Cover how an MCP server fits, the Settings tab, equal management by the captain and the first mate, secrets through the planned secret management, and access for the first mate and every crewmate. Also cover events that trigger automations. Propose it as one plan card, with the decisions and the pieces.", "userWords": "i'd like a fable crewmate to investigate how we might support gmail and google calendar integration in infinitary. look at how we supported github integration as reference. i'd like to design a core primitive called \"integrations\" in infinitary, and it should be scalable to handle other 3rd party data connections. a custom integration like our github is an integration. a MCP server should also be treated as an integration. there should be an \"integrations\" tab in settings. both user and first mate agent should have the same acsess to manage it. secrets used to access the external data should be handled by the planned secret management  capability. once an integration is added, both first mate agent and all crewmate agents should have access to it. it should also hook up to our automation system, as the 3rd party data sources may emit events that can trigger our automation - such as \"whenever an email arrives in my gmail, run a triage prompt...\". have the fable crewmate design this thoroughly with clean elegant architecture and scalability in mind.", "constraints": "read-only; free: The planned secret management is the saved plan behind INF task 92c99f1f \"Build the saved built-in secret management\"; read its basis plan. Never open a real secret or keychain.", "expectedKind": "plan", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-775 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance