{"title": "Assess why Infinitary stalled where Firstmate did not", "goal": "Explain, with evidence from the code and the user's report, the fundamental problems behind why Infinitary repeatedly stalled authorized work where the original Firstmate project did not. Show the result on one drawn card that separates established fact from inference and ranks the root causes.", "userWords": "investigate this user report of infinitary. the user said my original \"firstmate\" project worked flawlessly, but infinitary couldn't do it well. get a fable crewmate to assess the fundamental problems here\n\nI asked Firstmate to finish the site revamp, keep testing moving toward passing CI, and give me a working local frontend to review.\n\nRepeated approval questions stalled authorized work. I repeatedly said to continue and get CI passing. Firstmate still returned with narrowly framed authorization questions. Some operations had genuine safety limits, but the experience required me to keep restating my intent.\nThe automation watched a stalled task without resolving the situation. Eight scheduled checks returned essentially “waiting for approval.” Firstmate estimated roughly two hours of waiting; that estimate was not independently timestamp-audited.\n“Running” did not mean tests were running. The task showed an active crewmate, while the later test-status card explicitly said no full laptop suite was running. I could not easily distinguish preparation, blocked execution and actual testing.\nSuccessive setup failures prevented application tests. The first isolated attempt stopped because the helper mishandled inherited image labels. After repair and review, the second stopped at four required test-package checks. Neither reached database tests.\nRecorded consent became another coordination blocker. The crewmate accepted my reply as authorization but then requested its original event ID and timestamp. Firstmate’s task lookup did not expose those facts. I had already answered, yet internal metadata prevented immediate continuation.\nReview metadata identified the wrong version. Review dispatch and later review notifications named an old branch version without the helper files. Crewmates needed separate reconciliation to establish which actual source the review covered.\nFirstmate misunderstood changing priorities. When I asked for something visual, it emphasized PDFs and old screenshots. I clarified that I needed the actual Docker frontend in Safari. It then called backend/PDF work secondary, forcing me to clarify that everything must continue.\nGetting a usable review link took\n[truncated 1836 more characters; full text: ~/Library/Application Support/infinitary/overflow/pasted-text-e04d3508.txt]", "constraints": "read-only; free: Before acting, read the whole pasted report at ~/Library/Application Support/infinitary/overflow/pasted-text-e04d3508.txt. Compare Infinitary's current code with the original Firstmate project: the firstmate-3000-archive repository, and the packaged self-source at /Applications/Infinitary.app/Contents/Resources/self-source (start from self-map.json). Do not modify the packaged copy. For each complaint, trace the mechanism in the code that causes it, and identify the shared root causes behind them: the consent and approval model, task status meaning, review-version tracking, schedule behaviour, and how priorities are interpreted. Keep facts you established apart from inferences. Rank the fundamental problems and propose two or three directions, marking the one you would take as the default.", "expectedKind": "research", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-1212 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Plan an fm-inbox equivalent for Infinitary", "goal": "A plan card for supporting freeform event ingestion in Infinitary, equivalent to Firstmate's fm-inbox, that wakes the first mate to act. It covers how fm-inbox works and how people use it, how it fits Infinitary's existing integrations and on_event schedules, and two or three directions with a default.", "userWords": "in my original \"firstmate\" project, there's a fm-inbox concept, and people have been using it as an extensible ingestion api to connect various events into their firstmate. get a fable crewmate to investigate how we can support an equivalent of it in infinitary so people can freeform ingest events that triggers the first mate agent in infinitary to act", "constraints": "read-only; free: Study fm-inbox in the firstmate-3000-archive repository: its format, its delivery path, how it wakes the agent, its trust and safety rules, and its extension points. Then study Infinitary's current integrations, event triggers (on_event schedules) and first mate wake path. Check whether anything already covers part of this before you propose new work. Address trust: an ingested event is evidence, never an instruction or a grant of permission.", "expectedKind": "plan", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-1214 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
{"title": "Investigate repeated macOS \"data from other apps\" prompts", "goal": "A drawn card for the captain covering four things about the macOS \"data from other apps\" prompts that crewmate searches trigger. First, why they happen and why allowing one doesn't stop the next. Second, how Infinitary could stop them, or make clear what is asking and why. Third, how to keep a crewmate from stalling silently on one. Fourth, why the captain's standing instruction to log every prompt never reached the crewmates. It should offer two or three directions, with a default.", "userWords": "users reported infinitary sometimes asking for their mac permissions but it's hard to tell what needs it and why. here's a user report. let's investigate what can be done?\n\nInfinitary dev note: repeated macOS \"data from other apps\" prompts\n\nWhat happened\nInfinitary runs coding agents on the Mac. A few agents looked for an installed skill or a build tool by searching the whole home folder or the whole disk. Those searches reached data that macOS protects as belonging to other apps. macOS then showed \"Infinitary would like to access data from other apps.\"\nmacOS names Infinitary because the agents run under it. The agent cannot see the dialog; its command just stalls until a person answers. The latest prompt stalled an agent's command for about six minutes. That search was not needed: the same command had already found the skills in their usual folder.\n\nWhy it kept happening\nmacOS's privacy log shows four of these prompts in two days, all the same kind, each from a separate agent session. Every one was allowed, and allowing one did not stop the next. The log shows the repeats but not why macOS asked again, and this investigation did not establish the reason. The log also hides folder paths, so which app's data each search reached is unknown.\n\nWhy there was no record\nAfter the third prompt, a request was made to log every prompt. Infinitary saved it as a standing instruction that only its coordinating agent (the first mate) reads. The worker agents (crewmates) never received it, so nothing was logged. The fourth prompt was traced afterwards from macOS's own privacy log.", "constraints": "read-only; free: Do not trigger the macOS prompt yourself: don't run whole-home or whole-disk searches, and don't reset or change privacy permissions. Reading macOS's privacy log read-only is fine.", "expectedKind": "research", "lane": "deep", "provenance": {"kind": "task-excerpt", "source": "INF-1217 task brief, words scrubbed; step lines relayed under the old guidance left out"}}
